# Docker + Argo CD Deployment Workflow (Frontend)

## 1. Make Changes to Your Application

Update your frontend code (for example, add a new button or modify the UI).

---

## 2. Build a New Docker Image

For the first release:

```bash
docker build -t harshparmar009/uptime-frontend:v1 .
```

For future releases:

```bash
docker build -t harshparmar009/uptime-frontend:v2 .
```

```bash
docker build -t harshparmar009/uptime-frontend:v3 .
```

Use a new version tag (`v2`, `v3`, etc.) whenever you want to deploy updated code.

---

## 3. Verify the Image Exists

List local Docker images:

```bash
docker images
```

Example:

```
REPOSITORY                         TAG
harshparmar009/uptime-frontend     v1
```

---

## 4. Test the Image Locally (Optional)

Run the container:

```bash
docker run -p 8081:80 harshparmar009/uptime-frontend:v1
```

Open:

```
http://localhost:8081
```

---

## 5. Push the Image to Docker Hub

Log in (if needed):

```bash
docker login
```

Push the image:

```bash
docker push harshparmar009/uptime-frontend:v1
```

---

## 6. Update `deployment.yaml`

Example:

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: uptime-frontend

spec:
  replicas: 1

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: uptime-frontend

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: uptime-frontend

    spec:
      containers:
        - name: uptime-frontend
          image: harshparmar009/uptime-frontend:v1
          ports:
            - containerPort: 80
```

When deploying a new version, change only the image tag:

```yaml
image: harshparmar009/uptime-frontend:v2
```

---

## 7. Commit and Push to GitHub

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Deploy frontend v1"
git push
```

For later versions:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Deploy frontend v2"
git push
```

---

## 8. Argo CD Synchronization

Argo CD continuously watches the Git repository.

When it detects that `deployment.yaml` has changed (for example, `v1` → `v2`), it synchronizes Kubernetes automatically (or when you click **Sync**, depending on your configuration).

---

## 9. Kubernetes Deployment

Kubernetes pulls the new Docker image from Docker Hub and performs a rolling update so the application runs the latest version.

---

# Overall Workflow

```
Developer changes code
          │
          ▼
Build Docker image (v2)
          │
          ▼
Push image to Docker Hub
          │
          ▼
Update deployment.yaml
(image: v1 → v2)
          │
          ▼
Commit & Push to GitHub
          │
          ▼
Argo CD detects Git change
          │
          ▼
Argo CD syncs Kubernetes
          │
          ▼
Kubernetes pulls v2 image
          │
          ▼
Updated application is running
```

# Important Notes

* Argo CD watches **Git**, not your source code directly.
* Kubernetes runs **Docker images**, not your project files.
* For every application update, create and push a **new image tag** (`v1`, `v2`, `v3`, ...).
* Avoid relying on `latest` for production deployments; explicit version tags make updates and rollbacks easier to manage.
